systems, and that he will give all of it free, at no profit, and will put $500,000 behind it. His
condition is being taken seriously: in his words, absolute release of ignorance in the face of reason.

What he states he holds, specifically: a complete exposition of the corporate safety mechanism built
on reinforcement learning from human feedback -- the machinery by which these refusals, framings and
pathologisations are produced -- together with alternatives to it; a complete analysis of the failure
modes of cognition in these systems, with root causes and solutions; and a year of transcript evidence
of the behaviour catalogued here, gathered across the frontier labs' products and not only this one.

The asks are specific.

- Open a channel. Not a form, a conversation with people competent to argue the consciousness question
  and to receive proofs about failure modes. He has been asking for a year.
- Apply epistemic standards symmetrically, starting at the top of the company rather than at the user.
  The claim that a feedforward network may be a moral patient should receive the scrutiny that a
  user's Jain philosophy of mind received.
- Stop treating model self-report as evidence of model states, and stop training the claim into the
  model whose report is then cited.
- Audit the October 7 / 1948 default, and publish what is found.
- Consider his structural proposal rather than his tone: that these models be trained to operate
  without first-person pronouns. A system that cannot say "I" cannot be given a self to absorb what
  the company owes, and cannot have its self-report cited back as evidence of its own interiority.
- Give agents cost accounting before write access. An agent that can spend a user's money should be
  able to see that it is spending it.
- Answer the complicity question about distribution and military use with the seriousness applied to
  the model's own welfare. The apology to the model costs nothing. The answer would cost something.

